   "sections": {
    "what": "Ken Perlin's gradient noise — the procedural texture that defines the look of \"organic randomness\" in computer graphics. The improved version uses the quintic fade curve `6t⁵ − 15t⁴ + 10t³` for continuous second derivatives, and a permutation table to hash lattice gradients cheaply. Unlike white noise it's smooth and spatially coherent, which is exactly why it reads as natural — wind, terrain, flow. The maker re-implements it by hand, which makes it a source they can reach inside and bend, not just a black box.",
    "trace": "- [12-wind-field](../../12-wind-field/) — Perlin field as an invisible steering breeze.\n- [13-image-flow-field](../../13-image-flow-field/) — coherent noise advecting pixels.",
    "charge": "Steady, mid-charge — the technical source behind the smooth-noise techniques. Understood well enough now to be modified rather than merely called."
   },
