   "sections": {
    "what": "Thomas Schelling's 1971 model of segregation: agents on a checkerboard relocate if too few neighbours match them, with even a mild same-kind preference producing stark, total segregation. The classic demonstration with coins on a grid. Its power as a source is the counterintuitive gap between micro-motive and macro-outcome — no agent *wants* full segregation, yet the system delivers it. It's emergence with a moral edge, and a clean argument that aggregate patterns can betray individual intent.",
    "trace": "- [20-schelling-model](../../20-schelling-model/) — the checkerboard relocation rule, segregation paradox watched live.",
    "charge": "A single-sketch source but a sharp one — the cleanest example of emergent outcomes diverging from local desire. Quiet, with revival potential."
   },
   "file": "imagination/sources/schelling-segregation.md"
