   "sections": {
    "what": "Alan Turing's 1952 insight that two diffusing, reacting chemicals can break symmetry and form stable spatial patterns — spots, stripes, the morphology of animal coats. The Gray-Scott system is the most-used computational instance: two coupled PDEs whose feed and kill rates, tuned slightly, span an astonishing variety of patterns from mitosis-like blobs to coral mazes. It's emergence in a *continuous* medium, the chemistry counterpart to discrete cellular automata, and a reminder that pattern can be a physical inevitability.",
    "trace": "- [52-reaction-diffusion](../../52-reaction-diffusion/) — Gray-Scott feed/kill parameters explored as a pattern space.",
    "charge": "Medium — one sketch deep but the parameter space is vast and only sampled. A natural revival source for the pattern-formation thread."
   },
   "file": "imagination/sources/turing-gray-scott.md"
